\section{Experimental Results}
\label{sec:selection_results}
Table~\ref{tab:selection_results} compares the selected configurations with the library defaults on $54$ test transforms: one, two and three dimensions, single and double precision, and nine densities from $0.125$ to $32$ points per fine-grid cell, each with at least $4.2$ million points and an upsampling factor of $2$. The points are distributed uniformly and, on $20$ threads, also exponentially, which clusters them towards one center of the grid and so departs from the uniform distribution the estimates assume; the same selected configurations are used for both.  Each entry is the geometric mean over the transforms of different densities.

\begin{table}[ht]
    \centering
    \small
    \begin{tabular}{lrrrr}
        \toprule
        & \multicolumn{4}{c}{geometric average speed-up} \\
        \cmidrule(lr){2-5}
        & \multicolumn{3}{c}{uniform distribution} & exponential distribution \\
        \cmidrule(lr){2-4} \cmidrule(lr){5-5}
        transforms & $20$ threads & $10$ threads & $1$ thread & $20$ threads \\
        \midrule
        1D double & $1.99$ & $1.47$ & $1.11$ & $1.76$ \\
        1D single & $2.10$ & $1.56$ & $1.19$ & $1.73$ \\
        2D double & $1.30$ & $1.15$ & $1.09$ & $1.39$ \\
        2D single & $1.45$ & $1.27$ & $1.08$ & $1.66$ \\
        3D double & $1.07$ & $0.99$ & $0.95$ & $0.97$ \\
        3D single & $1.13$ & $1.00$ & $0.96$ & $0.97$ \\
        \midrule
        all $54$ & $1.45$ & $1.22$ & $1.06$ & $1.37$ \\
        \bottomrule
    \end{tabular}
    \caption{Speed-up of sort plus spreading over the defaults.}
    \label{tab:selection_results}
\end{table}

The gains are largest in one dimension, $1.99$ to $2.10$ times, and smallest in three dimensions, only $1.07$ to $1.13$ times, where no bin larger than the default fits in L1d, so the procedure keeps the default $16 \times 4 \times 4$. Of the eight transforms that became slower on $20$ threads, six are of this kind, by at most $6\%$: the selected $S_{\max}$ is large enough to leave each thread only a few subproblems, so the work is unevenly divided among the threads, an effect the traffic estimate does not contain. The other two are sparse two-dimensional transforms in double precision, $1\%$ and $12\%$ slower, for which the estimate favours a configuration that the measurement does not.

With fewer threads the gains shrink. Spreading is then limited by latency and computation rather than by bandwidth, so savings in DRAM traffic no longer show in the run time, and in three dimensions, where the bins do not change at all, the selection is no better than the defaults.

\begin{table}[ht]
    \centering
    \small
    \begin{tabular}{lrrrr}
        \toprule
        & \multicolumn{4}{c}{geometric average throughput improvement} \\
        \cmidrule(lr){2-5}
        & \multicolumn{3}{c}{uniform distribution} & exponential distribution \\
        \cmidrule(lr){2-4} \cmidrule(lr){5-5}
        transforms & $20$ threads & $10$ threads & $1$ thread & $20$ threads \\
        \midrule
        1D double & $1.99$ & $1.42$ & $1.11$ & $1.77$ \\
        1D single & $2.04$ & $1.50$ & $1.19$ & $1.80$ \\
        2D double & $1.23$ & $1.09$ & $1.09$ & $1.25$ \\
        2D single & $1.32$ & $1.22$ & $1.08$ & $1.19$ \\
        3D double & $1.03$ & $1.05$ & $0.96$ & $1.18$ \\
        3D single & $1.06$ & $1.06$ & $0.97$ & $1.12$ \\
        \midrule
        all $54$ & $1.39$ & $1.21$ & $1.06$ & $1.36$ \\
        \bottomrule
    \end{tabular}
    \caption{Throughput gain of sort plus spreading over the defaults.}
    \label{tab:selection_work}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:selection_work} measures the CPU time of sorting and spreading, the time the threads spend doing actual work. The gains are significant in this case as well, and three-dimensional transforms gain more than in Table~\ref{tab:selection_results}. The wall-clock time is lost because the selection chooses large subproblems that divide the work unevenly: the threads do less work in total, but those that finish early wait for the one holding the most expensive subproblem.

\begin{table}[ht]
    \centering
    \small
    \begin{tabular}{lrrrr}
        \toprule
        & \multicolumn{4}{c}{geometric average throughput improvement} \\
        \cmidrule(lr){2-5}
        & \multicolumn{3}{c}{uniform distribution} & exponential distribution \\
        \cmidrule(lr){2-4} \cmidrule(lr){5-5}
        transforms & $20$ threads & $10$ threads & $1$ thread & $20$ threads \\
        \midrule
        \multicolumn{5}{l}{\emph{$S_{\max}$ alone, against the library defaults}} \\
        1D double & $1.04$ & $1.00$ & $1.01$ & $1.01$ \\
        1D single & $0.97$ & $0.99$ & $0.99$ & $0.98$ \\
        2D double & $1.11$ & $1.04$ & $1.03$ & $1.16$ \\
        2D single & $1.04$ & $1.10$ & $1.00$ & $1.01$ \\
        3D double & $1.03$ & $1.05$ & $0.96$ & $1.18$ \\
        3D single & $1.06$ & $1.06$ & $0.97$ & $1.12$ \\
        \cmidrule(lr){1-5}
        all $54$ & $1.04$ & $1.04$ & $0.99$ & $1.08$ \\
        \bottomrule
    \end{tabular}
    \caption{Throughput gain of sort plus spreading with only $S_{\max}$ selected.}
    \label{tab:selection_split}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:selection_split} separates the gains from selecting $S_{\max}$ from those of selecting the bin dimensions. On one thread only the bin dimensions improve performance, while selecting $S_{\max}$ changes the time by no more than noise. In three dimensions the algorithm finds no better bin dimensions, because no larger bin fits in L1d, so only one- and two-dimensional transforms benefit from selecting them.


